069 "q-156607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120150 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y = 1 - 2x$ in $R^{2}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $R^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120150 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d2b6331-122d-5166-a305-241f7f5b32d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120151 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120151 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120151 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120151 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120151 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120151 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe544997-b5f0-5f74-9a6d-c604c1790f93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120151 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-y=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -a+(-b) \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-y=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-a) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-y=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1061 41645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f009ee8-4a5e-5da7-b539-b2991cbd9d04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1061 41646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7eb434d-3210-5f2f-b5ec-18781d0fa02d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1061 41660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "911d42c3-5fd0-515e-abcd-cfaab6aa6926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1061 41667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b4b4898-6c15-52d9-96fe-d57251b08369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1061 41674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "124c571f-28f0-56fa-a68d-76ef241f4618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1061 41675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d8c8e20-696b-5df3-beaf-a78962ce0420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1064 "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words:\n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n$$L = \\left\\{ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: y=3x \\right\\}$$\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 3x\\end{aligned}],$ where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above.\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ If we compute their sum, we get $$\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, for any scalar $c$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set\n\n$$B=\\left\\{ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: y=x+1 \\right\\}$$\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1065 6853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee2c580c-5386-5e06-9b58-e9e1dd398cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is $$\\mathbf{d}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] - [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] .$$ This means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is $$\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ If we compute their sum, we get $$\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1065 6859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb0bb214-7f56-557a-82f7-baeef815457e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1067 "Lines as Subspaces of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1064 "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 4x-2y\\end{aligned}], \\qquad x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}.$$\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above.\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$ If we compute their sum, we get $$\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, for any scalar $c$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1065 6860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3fcbfc6c-e5f6-5344-8ddd-779b625a03ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1067 "Planes as Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272628 #edb/ref 1080