417] [17592186274981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274981 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274981 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274981 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186274259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274981 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c5d26109-e4a8-5040-b8ff-5e1ca19040b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186272682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275002 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275002 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275002 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186273989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275002 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d137125c-728c-5b38-a1dd-d174752da874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275058 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186272841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275058 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275058 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275058 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275058 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275058 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275058 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275058 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186273816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275058 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ef527d9b-d04c-5f20-b418-bb16551ec425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275389 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275389 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2y=3x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ca63685-40ca-5dbf-8721-4e6b3a5a2913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275389 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $2y=3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =3x \\\\ 2(0) & =3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a+\\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(\\frac{3}{2}a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275399 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275399 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275399 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275399 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275399 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99eda82b-48bc-5827-9c44-f2725d46a5d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275399 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (4)-2(0)+2(1) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376041 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376041 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376041 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376041 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376041 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376041 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376041 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376041 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186376045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376041 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "734e052a-7544-5481-b6fa-03e39bd162c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376042 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376042 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376042 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "735b9f06-16c3-525e-8e71-064577376d3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376043 #edb/ref 1080 "II and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376043 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376043 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "89d90843-0e5b-5282-91a5-459c23d358b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376044 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376044 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376044 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c7681ca-cf09-59b0-9214-97acea3380ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376045 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376045 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376045 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8c102d18-68ae-569a-88c9-f7a90ad5be2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376046 #edb/ref 1080 "IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376046 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376046 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3bb7d32d-49b3-5fe0-94cf-c277de74c950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376047 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77f2cd70-5c51-5abc-8136-b13970866790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376047 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376048 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376048 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376048 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 175921